/**

@page page_exahype_main_function ExaHyPE's main function

\tableofcontents

Every Peano 4 application needs exactly one file pair that hosts `main()`
(@see @ref page_architecture_home "the Peano 4 architecture page" for the
general Hollywood-principle control flow this main function drives). For a
plain peano4.api.Project, this pair is produced by
peano4.api.runner.DefaultSequence. exahype2.api.Project instead installs its
own generator for this step, exahype2.api.DefaultSimulationMain, via the
project's `mainfile_generator` attribute.


# Project.mainfile_generator

exahype2.api.Project.__init__() sets

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
self.mainfile_generator = DefaultSimulationMain(self)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

and generate_Peano4_project() later calls
`self.mainfile_generator.construct_output(peano_project)`.
exahype2.api.DefaultSimulationMain is a
exahype2.api.Project.Generator (the same base class used for
exahype2.api.SolverToCPPFiles on the solver side, @see
@ref page_exahype_design_philosophy "design philosophy"): its
construct_output() replaces `peano_project.main` with
`_ExaHyPEMainFileSequence`, an adapter that speaks the
`construct_output(output, main_name)` protocol
peano4.api.Project.generate() expects from a project's `main` attribute, but
fills the generated header/implementation pair with ExaHyPE-flavoured
content instead of the mesh-agnostic Peano 4 default.

Since `mainfile_generator` is a plain attribute, an application can swap in
its own Generator to produce a different main() - e.g. to drive several
solvers through a custom control flow - without having to touch anything
else in exahype2.api.Project.


# What the generated main() currently does

`_ExaHyPEMainFileSequence` emits a header that just declares `int main(int
argc, char** argv);`, plus an implementation that

- calls `peano4::initParallelEnvironment()`, `peano4::fillLookupTables()`
  and the generated `repositories::DataRepository::initDatatypes()`,
- calls `peano4::initSingletons()` with the project's domain offset, domain
  size and periodic boundary condition flags,
- and, symmetrically, shuts all three back down again before returning.

Between the init and the shutdown block, the generated main() does not yet
drive any mesh traversals: the task graph compiler
(@ref page_exahype_compute_graph "lowering into a compute graph") only
decides which Observers exist per algorithmic phase and in which order they
may run - it does not yet say how a whole phase's Observers together realise
the actual `peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::traverse()` sweeps that make up
the simulation loop (setup, then repeated time steps interleaved with
plotting, until the terminal time is reached). This orchestration is the
next piece to land.

*/
